+# Decision: Self-hosted WebSockets for committed-change delivery
+
+## Context
+
+MarketBoard must deliver committed stall-status changes to as many as 800 simultaneous staff browsers, including opening-time bursts of 20 edits per second. Connected browsers should see changes within 15 seconds under normal connectivity, and a browser must recover the latest board after up to 20 minutes disconnected.
+
+Editors already submit commands over HTTPS. The delivery choice therefore concerns how committed revisions reach browsers; it does not change the authoritative command path or the one-board-per-event model. The choice also shapes the change store, delivery service, and browser recovery behavior, so reversing it would affect all three components.
+
+The existing goals set the baseline every option must meet. Selection turns on update latency, independence from realtime vendors, operating burden and cost, and the downstream complexity each option creates.
+
+## Decision Elements
+
+- **Update latency and responsiveness — major.** Prefer delivery that makes committed changes visible promptly rather than merely satisfying the 15-second ceiling. This supports the operational value of a shared live board.
+- **Realtime vendor independence — major.** Prefer infrastructure the team controls over recurring provider fees and coupling to a provider's credentials, APIs, and subscription model. This applies the tenet to prefer self-hosted realtime delivery over provider coupling when the team can operate the required connections and recovery.
+- **Operating burden and cost — moderate.** Weigh connection capacity, reconnect failures, monitoring, repeated read load, and recurring service fees. A lower operational burden remains valuable, but does not outweigh both major criteria together.
+- **Downstream implementation and recovery complexity — moderate.** Account for the storage, publication, subscription, reconnect, and catch-up mechanisms each option requires across the three affected components.
+
+## Options in the Domain
+
+### Five-second HTTPS polling
+
+Browsers request changes over HTTPS every five seconds. The server answers ordinary request/response queries using a revision cursor, and each browser runs a polling loop. No persistent delivery sessions or realtime provider are required.
+
+#### Analysis
+
+- **Update latency and responsiveness: weak.** Updates wait for the next poll and request completion. This can meet the 15-second goal but adds several seconds of avoidable lag compared with push delivery.
+- **Realtime vendor independence: strong.** Delivery uses self-hosted HTTPS infrastructure and introduces no realtime provider dependency or recurring realtime vendor bill.
+- **Operating burden and cost: partial.** Stateless requests avoid persistent connection capacity and reconnect operations. In exchange, 800 active browsers polling every five seconds generate an average of 160 delivery requests per second, including periods when nothing has changed.
+- **Downstream implementation and recovery complexity: strong.** The store needs cursor queries, delivery remains request/response, and browsers need a polling loop. The mechanism is comparatively simple, although repeated reads and cursor behavior still require design and testing.
+
+### Self-hosted WebSockets
+
+The team operates persistent WebSocket connections between browsers and its own delivery service. Committed revisions are published to connected browsers as they occur. Browsers reconnect after interruption and use retained revisions to catch up.
+
+#### Analysis
+
+- **Update latency and responsiveness: strong.** Connected browsers receive pushed changes without waiting for a polling interval.
+- **Realtime vendor independence: strong.** The realtime path uses infrastructure and protocols controlled by the team, with no realtime vendor bill or provider-specific browser subscription model.
+- **Operating burden and cost: weak.** The team must size and monitor connection capacity, diagnose dropped connections, operate reconnect behavior, and ensure committed changes are published reliably. It avoids recurring realtime provider fees but accepts substantial connection operations work.
+- **Downstream implementation and recovery complexity: weak.** The store must retain replayable revisions and publish committed changes reliably; delivery must manage sessions and publication; browsers must implement reconnect and catch-up behavior. These obligations span all three components.
+
+### Hosted realtime service
+
+A hosted provider accepts published changes and maintains browser subscriptions. The delivery service wraps the provider's API, and browsers use the provider's subscription model.
+
+#### Analysis
+
+- **Update latency and responsiveness: strong.** Provider-managed subscriptions push updates promptly without a polling delay.
+- **Realtime vendor independence: weak.** The design acquires recurring fees, provider credentials, and coupling to external publication and subscription APIs. Provider behavior becomes part of the realtime delivery path.
+- **Operating burden and cost: partial.** The provider reduces the team's connection-capacity and socket-reconnect operations. Recurring fees and provider-specific operational concerns replace part of that work.
+- **Downstream implementation and recovery complexity: partial.** The provider supplies subscription machinery, but the store still needs publication reconciliation, delivery needs a provider wrapper, and browsers must integrate the provider's subscription and recovery model.
+
+## Selection
+
+MarketBoard uses **self-hosted WebSockets** for committed-change delivery.
+
+This is the only option with a strong fit for both major criteria: it delivers updates promptly and keeps the realtime path independent of a service provider. Polling has the same independence and simpler operations, but its polling delay gives up responsiveness. A hosted service preserves responsiveness and reduces connection operations, but introduces the recurring fees and provider coupling the team wants to avoid.
+
+The decision turns on the tenet to prefer self-hosted realtime delivery over provider coupling when the required connections and recovery are operable. The team accepts the operating and implementation burden as the price of low-latency, vendor-independent updates; that burden is not treated as incidental or eliminated by the choice.
+
+Committing to this approach obligates the change store to provide replayable revisions and reliable publication, delivery to manage connection capacity and socket sessions, and browser sync to reconnect and catch up after missed revisions. Detailed publication, replay, and reconnect protocols remain design work for those components. HTTPS remains the command path, and this decision does not prescribe a delivery fallback or rule out ordinary HTTPS reads for board recovery.
